<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frombork</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Frauenburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frombork (German Frauenburg) is a small town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula-lagoon">Vistula Lagoon</a>. From the 13th century it was the seat of the cathedral chapter of Warmia, whose fortified Gothic cathedral stands on a hill above the town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a> lived there as a canon for much of his life, wrote his work on the motion of the heavens there, and was buried in the cathedral in 1543.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frombork.jpg" alt="Frombork" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: QuercusGd · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frombok_Katedra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Frombork lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula-lagoon">Vistula Lagoon</a> in Braniewo County, in the historical region of Warmia. The town was founded as a defensive stronghold on an Old Prussian site, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, granted control of the region by the imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-rimini">Golden Bull of Rimini</a>, conquered it. According to a local legend, its name, Frauenburg in German, honours Gertruda, the widow of the stronghold's lord, who offered the settlement to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. The name is a common one, since it usually marks a fortified chapel, church or monastery dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The village is first mentioned in a document of 1278 signed by Bishop Heinrich Fleming, and on 8 July 1310 Bishop Eberhard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a> granted the town <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> city rights. The Brick Gothic cathedral was built between 1329 and 1388. The town was plundered and burned in 1414 during the Hunger War, joined the anti-Teutonic Prussian Confederation in 1440, and was recognised as part of the Polish Kingdom by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a> in 1466. It was devastated again in 1520, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-teutonic-war-1519-1521">Polish–Teutonic War</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a> lived and worked there as a canon in 1512–16 and from 1522 until his death. In 1519 he wrote to the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> asking for help against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>; the letter was intercepted, and the Knights took and burned the town after Copernicus and other canons had left. He wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-revolutionibus-orbium-coelestium">De revolutionibus</a> orbium cœlestium in Frombork, and after its publication in 1543 he died there and was buried in the cathedral. Between 1626 and 1635 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> occupied the town and sent many cultural treasures, including Copernicus's manuscripts, to Sweden. Prussia took the town in 1772 and it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Heavily damaged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, the town passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1945, regained its city rights in 1959, and was rebuilt in 1966–1973 by Polish Boy Scouts and others, in time for the 500th anniversary of Copernicus's birth. The fortified Cathedral Hill holds the cathedral, the Copernicus Tower and the Old Bishop's Palace, which houses the Nicolaus Copernicus Museum, listed as a Historic Monument of Poland. The cathedral is the seat of the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Warmia. Archaeologists found a grave believed to be Copernicus's in 2005; DNA tests confirmed it in 2008, and he was reburied in the cathedral in 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frombork” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The province took in Pomerelia with the mouth of the Vistula and the city of Danzig, the Chełmno and Michałów Lands with Thorn, the Prince-Bishopric of Warmia with <b>Frauenburg</b>, Heilsberg and Allenstein, and the Marienburger Land with Marienburg and the city of Elbing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Nicolaus Copernicus, a canon of <b>Frombork</b> in Warmia, published On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres, which placed the Sun near the centre of the universe and made the Earth a planet, turning daily on its axis and circling the Sun each year; an unsigned preface by the…</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishops made Lidzbark their residence and the cathedral chapter settled at <b>Frombork</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-warmia">Prince-Bishopric of Warmia</a></li></ul></section>
